Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Vmag (apparent visual magnitude): Brighter stars (lower Vmag) tend to be either closer or intrinsically luminous. However, giant stars can be bright or faint depending on distance; alone, Vmag is not a strong indicator of giant status without distance information.

- Plx (parallax): Larger parallax means the star is closer. Giants are often luminous but can be distant, so smaller parallax might indicate a giant if the star is bright intrinsically.

- e_Plx (error in parallax): High error reduces reliability of distance estimate; not directly linked to giant status but affects confidence in Amag.

- B-V (color index): Indicates temperature and spectral class. Giants often have certain B-V ranges depending on spectral type, typically redder (higher B-V) for cooler giants.

- SpType (spectral type): The luminosity class in SpType (e.g., V for main sequence, III for giants) directly indicates whether the star is a giant or not.

- Amag (absolute magnitude): Intrinsic brightness; giants have lower (brighter) absolute magnitudes compared to dwarfs of the same spectral type. Lower Amag values indicate more luminous stars (giants).

