Answer:  
Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Vmag (apparent magnitude): Brighter stars (lower Vmag) can be either giants or dwarfs, but giants tend to be intrinsically brighter, so Vmag alone is less decisive without distance.
- Plx (parallax): Larger parallax means the star is closer. Giants tend to be luminous and seen at greater distances with smaller parallax.
- e_Plx (error in parallax): Higher error may reduce confidence in derived distance or absolute magnitude, but not directly related to giant/dwarf classification.
- B-V (color index): Indicates star temperature/color. Giants often have redder colors (higher B-V), but color alone doesn't confirm giant status.
- SpType (spectral type): The luminosity class in spectral type is critical; "III" or "III/IV" indicates giants or subgiants, "V" indicates main-sequence (dwarf) stars.
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Giants have lower (more negative) absolute magnitudes (brighter intrinsically) than dwarfs.

Step 2. Based on above and examples, 10 different conditions for each class:

10 different conditions for class "0" (not giant star):
- SpType is in [G1V, F3V, F7V, A0V, K5V, B8V, B9V]
- SpType is in categories ending with "V" (main sequence)
- Amag >= 10
- Amag is within range [15, 20]
- Plx >= 5
- Plx is within range [5, 25]
- B-V <= 0.5
- B-V is within range [0, 0.6]
- Vmag >= 6
- e_Plx <= 1

10 different conditions for class "1" (giant star):
- SpType is in [K2III, K3III, G8III, B9III/IV, K1III, M0III]
- SpType contains "III" or "III/IV" (giant or subgiant luminosity class)
- Amag <= 15
- Amag is within range [10, 16]
- Plx < 5
- Plx is within range [0, 5]
- B-V >= 1.0
- B-V is within range [1.0, 1.5]
- Vmag <= 8.9
- e_Plx <= 1

These conditions reflect that giant stars generally have spectral types with luminosity class III or III/IV, lower absolute magnitudes (brighter intrinsically), smaller parallax (farther away), and redder colors (higher B-V). Non-giants tend to have luminosity class V, higher absolute magnitudes (fainter intrinsically), larger parallax (closer), and bluer colors (lower B-V).

